<|im_start|>user
Problem: During the World Cup, there are \( n \) different Panini stickers to collect. Marco's friends are trying to complete their collection, but nobody has a full set of stickers yet! A pair of his friends are said to be wholesome if their combined collection has at least one of each sticker. Marco knows the contents of everyone's collections, and wants to take them all to a restaurant for his birthday. However, he doesn't want any wholesome pairs sitting at the same table.

(i) Show that Marco might need to reserve at least \( n \) different tables.

(ii) Show that \( n \) tables will always be enough for Marco to achieve his goal.

Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.<|im_end|>
<|im_start|>assistant
<think>

</think>



Completion:
We are given a problem involving $ n $ different Panini stickers to collect. Marco has friends whose collections are partially complete. We are told that:

- A **pair** of friends is **wholesome** if together they have **all** $ n $ different stickers.
- Marco **wants** to **take** all of his friends to a **restaurant**, but he **wants to avoid** putting **any wholesome pair** at **the same table**.

We are to:
- (i) Show that Marco **might** need to reserve **at least $ n $** different tables.
- (ii) Show that **$ n $ tables** will always be **sufficient** for him to achieve his goal.

Let’s break this down step by step.

---

## **Part (i): Marco might need at least $ n $ different tables**

We are to show that **it is possible** that **$ n $ different tables** are needed — that is, **$ n $** might be a **lower bound** on the **number of tables** Marco might need.

### Goal:
Show that **it is possible** to have **no fewer than $ n $** tables required. We must **construct a scenario** where **each** table only has **one** person (not even one pair), and that no two people (friends) form a **wholesome** pair (i.e., **no two** friends together have **all** stickers).

Let’s analyze:

### Key Idea:
If **each** pair of friends **does not** form a **wholesome pair**, then **no two** people can be seated at the **same table**.

Why? Because **if two** friends **can** be seated at a **table** (i.e., they are at the same table), **then** the **pair** is **not wholesome**.

But **if** no two friends are **at the same table**, then **each person** has **only** their **own** table.

But that would **require** **at least $ n $** tables (each for one friend), since **you cannot have more than one person per table**, and you **have $ n $** people.

But here’s the twist — **it is possible that** **no pair** is **wholesome**, and **each** pair is **not** wholesome.

So:

Let’s now **construct** a specific setup where **no pair** is **wholesome**, and yet the **minimum number of tables** must be **at least $ n $**. That is, we can have **no pair** of two people who are **wholesome**, but we **still require** at least **n** people to be **seated at separate tables**.

So the point is that **if every pair is not wholesome**, then **every two people cannot sit together** — and so the **number of tables needed** is **exactly** the number of **people**, i.e., **at least $ n $**.

Therefore, **Marco might need $ n $ tables** — that is, the **worst-case** (or **possible scenario**) is that **no pair is wholesome**, but **each person must be alone**, requiring **at least $ n $** tables.

Thus, **part (i)** is **proven**.

---

## **Part (ii): $ n $ tables will always be enough**

We now show that, **under any scenario**, no more than **$ n $** tables are required, i.e., that **$ n $** tables **suffice**.

Again, the key is understanding what a **wholesome pair** is: **a pair** of people **together** having **all** the $ n $ stickers. So that pair **must not** be seated together.

We want to **avoid seating** any such pair **at the same table**.

So here’s the strategy:

We are to **color** the set of **n** people with **at most $ n $** **colors**, such that **no two** people who are **wholesome** (i.e., together have all $ n $ stickers) share **the same color**.

But wait — that **seems too abstract**.

Let’s take a **better approach** — the problem is now a coloring problem.

We are trying to **partition** the **set of $ n $** people (friends) into **at most $ n $** **tables** (i.e., subsets), **with the constraint**: **no two** people can be seated at the **same table** if they form a **wholesome pair**.

That is, we can **seat** people **at the same table** only **if they don't form a wholesome pair**.

So the **goal** is to **partition the $ n $ people** into subsets (tables), such that **no subset** has two
